%erg 0.1
Title: A throw inside render() must not permanently kill the sweep loop
Created: 2026-09-06
Author: claude
Blocked-by: 0689

--- log ---
2026-09-06T00:00Z claude created

--- body ---
## Context

Fable 5.1 deep-QA pass (2026-09-06). `publish()` calls `host.changed` ->
`render()`, which runs unguarded DOM work over `dialogs`, and is invoked
from inside the scheduler's `finally` block. If `render()` throws there
(realistic triggers: a dead XUL dialog object after its window closed,
`getElementById` returning null mid-render), `sitter.sweep()` itself
rejects. The bootstrap wrapper that reschedules the next sweep never
runs, so the sitter silently stops forever for the rest of the session
while `state.phase` still shows `waiting`/`extracting` -- indistinguishable
from working, from the outside.

Ticket 0689's diagnostic journal does not see this: no `sweep-start`/
`sweep-end` records exist today, and its heartbeat is silent whenever no
document is active (see ticket 0703) -- so this failure mode currently
produces zero trace anywhere, matching the author's own irreproducible
hang this morning.

## Work

- Wrap `render()`'s body (or at minimum the call from `publish()`) in a
  try/catch that cannot itself propagate.
- Wrap the sweep-rescheduling wrapper so it always reschedules the next
  sweep and always emits a record, regardless of whether the sweep body
  threw.
- Emit explicit `sweep-start`/`sweep-end` (success or error) events
  through 0689's journal so a hang here becomes diagnosable after the
  fact, not just prevented.

## Acceptance

- [ ] A synthetic `render()` throw does not prevent the next sweep from
      being scheduled 30s later.
- [ ] The journal (once 0689 lands) records the render failure and the
      subsequent successful reschedule.
- [ ] No existing test regresses.
